# The harness every spec file in this directory loads.
#
# Portland's language spec is the ADRs' promises, executable — the third
# oracle. The differential harness proves the seed and the compiler agree with each
# other; it cannot prove either agrees with what was *decided*, since a shared
# misreading of an ADR passes it. Every example cites the ADR it pins.
#
# Deliberately tiny, and built only from what the language has today: no
# classes, no exceptions, and no instance variables — none of which exist yet.
#
# The shape is rubyspec's, which `yield` is what made possible: `describe`
# names a subject, `specify` an example, `expect` compares. Nothing here counts
# and nothing here panics — a tally has to be kept somewhere, and only a block
# can reach an outer `mutable`, so `specify` and `expect`, being methods, can
# never count. `script/spec` counts the printed lines instead, which is also
# how mspec separates examples from formatters, and it is what lets a run
# report every failure rather than stopping at the first.
#
# This file lives here rather than being copied into each spec because the compiler
# learned `require_relative` ([#37]). Splitting the suite was the thing that
# asked for it.

# `describe` and `specify` print their own name and run the block they were
# handed. That is the whole harness.
#
# `specify` rather than rubyspec's more familiar `it`, and not by taste. `it`
# is an ordinary binding under the no-shadow rule (ADR 0017), so a file that
# defines `def it` contests the name with every implicit-`it` block in it —
# which would make ADR 0017 the one ADR this suite structurally cannot pin,
# a blind spot in the suite whose whole job is to have none. `specify` is
# mspec's own alias for `it`, so this borrows prior art rather than inventing.
#
# Fixtures belong inside the `describe` that uses them — a `specify` block
# closes over where it was written, so it sees them. There is no `before`, and
# for an immutable fixture there is nothing for one to do: the setup runs once,
# both examples share the value, and no example can corrupt it for the next,
# because values never mutate (ADR 0015).
#
# A `mutable` fixture is the exception, and it must go inside the one `specify`
# that needs it. A block rebinding an outer `mutable` is the accumulator pattern
# ADR 0001 licenses, and it works on both oracles — which means a `mutable`
# declared at `describe` level and rebound by one example is visible to the
# next, so the examples stop being independent and their order starts to matter.
# Nothing in the language will stop you; this comment is the only guard.
def describe(subject)
  puts subject
  yield
end

# `context` is `describe`'s twin, letter for letter. rspec's distinction —
# `describe` names a thing, `context` a circumstance or a home — is carried
# entirely by the word at the call site. A twin rather than an alias, because
# aliasing is runtime metaprogramming and Portland doesn't have any; writing
# the two lines out is the whole cost.
#
# Nesting works — a block is a closure, so an inner `describe`'s examples see
# every fixture of the `context` above them, and sibling blocks reuse fixture
# names freely because block locals die at `end`. The transcript stays flat,
# though: a method cannot know its depth, for the same no-state reason the
# tally lives in the runner, so nested subjects print at the margin.
def context(subject)
  puts subject
  yield
end

def specify(behavior)
  puts "- #{behavior}"
  yield
end

# A hole we owe, held in place: the example stays written where it belongs,
# prints one countable `* PENDING` line, and its block never runs — which is
# the only semantics available, not a taste choice. rspec's true `pending`
# runs the block and alerts when it starts passing; that needs a failure to
# be caught, and here `expect` prints rather than raises while a missing
# builtin panics the whole file. mspec's guards skip the same way.
#
# The body still has to *parse* on both oracles, since blocks parse whether
# or not they run. Three tiers, and the block is what tells them apart:
#
# - will build, body writable — `pending "desc" do ... end`: the example is
#   already written, and landing the feature means deleting one word.
# - will build, body unparseable (missing syntax) — `pending` with no block,
#   the unwritable spelling and its issue number carried in the string:
#   `pending "5_280 — underscored literals do not lex (#71)"`.
# - decided out, forever — a comment, never a `pending`: the inventory is a
#   to-do list, and a won't-fix inside it would rot its trustworthiness.
#
# `script/spec | grep PENDING` is the standing inventory of what is owed
# (#23).
def pending(behavior)
  puts "* PENDING #{behavior}"
end

# One countable line per failure, indented under the example it belongs to, so
# the runner can find it and a reader can see which example it came from
# without the message having to repeat the description.
#
# `inspect` rather than interpolation ([#38]): a failure message must tell
# `"ROSE"` from `:ROSE` from `ROSE`-the-output-of-something, and it must
# survive an expected nil — which interpolation cannot render at all, since
# absence has no methods, `to_s` included (ADR 0005/0006). A `shown` helper
# used to paper over exactly that, and this is the deletion its comment
# asked for.
#
# The chain is the sentence grammar. `expect(x).to(eq(y))` reads
# subject-verb-preposition-complement, where `expect(x, y)` — and every xUnit
# `assert_equal x, y` — is verb-object-object with nothing joining the two.
# `.to` is doing a preposition's job, the same instinct that puts `.of` and
# `.from` on constructor entry points (ADR 0031). The parens around the
# matcher are the one piece of ceremony left, and [#68] is the parse that
# would drop them: `expect(x).to eq(y)`.
struct Expectation
  actual

  def to(matcher)
    return if matcher.matches?(actual)

    puts "  FAIL #{matcher.failure_message(actual)}"
  end

  def not_to(matcher)
    return unless matcher.matches?(actual)

    puts "  FAIL #{matcher.negated_failure_message(actual)}"
  end
end

def expect(actual)
  Expectation.new(actual: actual)
end

# The matcher protocol is `matches?`, `failure_message`, and
# `negated_failure_message` — structural, not declared. Nothing checks that a
# matcher supplies all three until it is asked for one; type inference (#9)
# is what turns the convention into a checked shape, and a `trait` cannot do
# it today because each matcher's `matches?` is its own.
#
# Every matcher carries at least one field, because a struct needs one. That
# is not a workaround here: the field a stateless matcher would not have
# turns out to be the word its failure message wants.
struct EqualMatcher
  expected

  def matches?(actual)
    actual == expected
  end

  def failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{inspect(expected)}, got #{inspect(actual)}"
  end

  def negated_failure_message(actual)
    "expected anything but #{inspect(expected)}"
  end
end

def eq(expected)
  EqualMatcher.new(expected: expected)
end

# `eql` is `eql?` (#252), which tells `1` from `1.0` where `eq`'s `==` does
# not: `expect(1.0).to eq(1)` passes and `expect(1.0).to eql(1)` fails.
# ruby/spec writes `should eql(...)` wherever the type of a number matters.
struct EqlMatcher
  expected

  def matches?(actual)
    actual.eql?(expected)
  end

  def failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{inspect(actual)} to be eql? to #{inspect(expected)}"
  end

  def negated_failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{inspect(actual)} not to be eql? to #{inspect(expected)}"
  end
end

def eql(expected)
  EqlMatcher.new(expected: expected)
end

# The type matchers (#252): `be_an_instance_of(Integer)`, `be_a(String)`,
# and `be_an`, `be_a`'s twin. The test is `actual.class == type`. Ruby's
# would be `type === actual`, but here `===` type-tests only a type written
# literally, and a type held in a field compares by equality, as does a
# pinned `in ^type` (#255); `is_a?` and `instance_of?` are not defined. nil
# has no class to ask, so it is no type's instance. In Ruby `be_a` admits
# subclasses and `be_an_instance_of` does not; with no class hierarchy here
# the two answer the same, and only the words differ.
struct TypeMatcher
  type
  words

  def matches?(actual)
    !actual.nil? && actual.class == type
  end

  def failure_message(actual)
    found = if actual.nil? then "nil" else "a #{inspect(actual.class)}" end
    "expected #{inspect(actual)} to be #{words} #{inspect(type)}, but it is #{found}"
  end

  def negated_failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{inspect(actual)} not to be #{words} #{inspect(type)}"
  end
end

def be_an_instance_of(type)
  TypeMatcher.new(type: type, words: "an instance of")
end

def be_a(type)
  TypeMatcher.new(type: type, words: "a")
end

def be_an(type)
  TypeMatcher.new(type: type, words: "an")
end

# The string matchers (#252): `match(/l+/)`, `start_with("rose")`, and
# `end_with("city")`. One struct apiece, each one method call apart, as the
# predicate matchers below are. RSpec's `start_with` and `end_with` also
# take an array's leading elements; that waits for a row that wants it.
struct PatternMatcher
  pattern

  def matches?(actual)
    actual.match?(pattern)
  end

  def failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{inspect(actual)} to match #{inspect(pattern)}"
  end

  def negated_failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{inspect(actual)} not to match #{inspect(pattern)}"
  end
end

def match(pattern)
  PatternMatcher.new(pattern: pattern)
end

struct StartMatcher
  prefix

  def matches?(actual)
    actual.start_with?(prefix)
  end

  def failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{inspect(actual)} to start with #{inspect(prefix)}"
  end

  def negated_failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{inspect(actual)} not to start with #{inspect(prefix)}"
  end
end

def start_with(prefix)
  StartMatcher.new(prefix: prefix)
end

struct EndMatcher
  suffix

  def matches?(actual)
    actual.end_with?(suffix)
  end

  def failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{inspect(actual)} to end with #{inspect(suffix)}"
  end

  def negated_failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{inspect(actual)} not to end with #{inspect(suffix)}"
  end
end

def end_with(suffix)
  EndMatcher.new(suffix: suffix)
end

# `cover(value)` is `Range#cover?` (#252).
struct CoverMatcher
  value

  def matches?(actual)
    actual.cover?(value)
  end

  def failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{inspect(actual)} to cover #{inspect(value)}"
  end

  def negated_failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{inspect(actual)} not to cover #{inspect(value)}"
  end
end

def cover(value)
  CoverMatcher.new(value: value)
end

# `all(matcher)` (#252) applies a matcher to every element, as in
# `expect(values).to all(be_greater_than(0))`. Its failure names the first
# element that missed and carries that matcher's own message, where
# `expect(values.all? { … }).to be_true` could say only false.
struct AllMatcher
  matcher

  def matches?(actual)
    actual.all? { matcher.matches?(it) }
  end

  def failure_message(actual)
    elements = actual.to_a
    index = elements.find_index { !matcher.matches?(it) } or panic("all: every element passed")
    "expected all of #{inspect(actual)} to pass, but the element at index #{index} did not: #{matcher.failure_message(elements[index])}"
  end

  def negated_failure_message(actual)
    "expected some element of #{inspect(actual)} to fail: #{matcher.negated_failure_message(actual.to_a.first)}"
  end
end

def all(matcher)
  AllMatcher.new(matcher: matcher)
end

# `match_array(items)` (#252): the same elements, each as many times, in any
# order — a Set's elements included. RSpec's `contain_exactly(1, 2, 3)` is
# the same check with the items spread out, which waits on splats (ADR 0014).
struct ArrayMatcher
  items

  def matches?(actual)
    actual.to_a.tally == items.tally
  end

  def failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{inspect(actual)} to hold exactly #{inspect(items)}, in any order"
  end

  def negated_failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{inspect(actual)} not to hold exactly #{inspect(items)}, in any order"
  end
end

def match_array(items)
  ArrayMatcher.new(items: items)
end

# ruby/spec's `be_close(expected, tolerance)`: within the tolerance, strictly.
struct CloseMatcher
  expected
  tolerance

  def matches?(actual)
    (actual - expected).abs < tolerance
  end

  def failure_message(actual)
    "expected within #{inspect(tolerance)} of #{inspect(expected)}, got #{inspect(actual)}"
  end

  def negated_failure_message(actual)
    "expected anything but within #{inspect(tolerance)} of #{inspect(expected)}"
  end
end

def be_close(expected, tolerance)
  CloseMatcher.new(expected: expected, tolerance: tolerance)
end

# The comparison matchers (#250): `expect(reading).to be_greater_than(0)`
# rather than `expect(reading > 0).to be_true`, so a failure names both
# values instead of saying "expected true, got false". One struct for the
# four, since they differ only by the operator and the words for it.
#
# The spelled-out name is the matcher; the shorthand is its twin, a second
# `def` calling the first, as `context` twins `describe` — aliasing is
# runtime metaprogramming, which Portland doesn't have. RSpec spells these
# `be > 0`, `be >= 0`, and has no `be_greater_than`; these names are this
# harness's own, as `specify` is. `be` below refuses the operator form.
struct ComparisonMatcher
  relation
  expected

  def matches?(actual)
    case relation
    when :greater_than             then actual > expected
    when :greater_than_or_equal_to then actual >= expected
    when :less_than                then actual < expected
    when :less_than_or_equal_to    then actual <= expected
    end
  end

  def failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{inspect(actual)} to be #{words} #{inspect(expected)}"
  end

  def negated_failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{inspect(actual)} not to be #{words} #{inspect(expected)}"
  end

  def words
    relation.to_s.tr("_", " ")
  end
end

def be_greater_than(expected)
  ComparisonMatcher.new(relation: :greater_than, expected: expected)
end

def be_gt(expected)
  be_greater_than(expected)
end

def be_greater_than_or_equal_to(expected)
  ComparisonMatcher.new(relation: :greater_than_or_equal_to, expected: expected)
end

def be_gte(expected)
  be_greater_than_or_equal_to(expected)
end

def be_less_than(expected)
  ComparisonMatcher.new(relation: :less_than, expected: expected)
end

def be_lt(expected)
  be_less_than(expected)
end

def be_less_than_or_equal_to(expected)
  ComparisonMatcher.new(relation: :less_than_or_equal_to, expected: expected)
end

def be_lte(expected)
  be_less_than_or_equal_to(expected)
end

# RSpec's `be_between(min, max)`, inclusive at both ends as RSpec's is by
# default. RSpec's `.exclusive` modifier waits for a row that wants it.
struct BetweenMatcher
  minimum
  maximum

  def matches?(actual)
    actual >= minimum && actual <= maximum
  end

  def failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{inspect(actual)} to be between #{inspect(minimum)} and #{inspect(maximum)} (inclusive)"
  end

  def negated_failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{inspect(actual)} not to be between #{inspect(minimum)} and #{inspect(maximum)} (inclusive)"
  end
end

def be_between(minimum, maximum)
  BetweenMatcher.new(minimum: minimum, maximum: maximum)
end

# `be` exists only to refuse. Someone will reach for RSpec's
# `expect(x).to be > 5` — future us included — and without this the file
# dies on "'be' is not defined", which names nothing to write instead. It
# panics rather than printing a FAIL line because `be > 5` goes on to call
# `>` on whatever `be` answers, and a mistake in writing a spec should stop
# the file on its first run, not be counted. Don't delete it as dead code,
# and don't make it work: the spelled-out matchers are the house spelling.
def be
  panic("`be > x` is RSpec's operator form, which this harness does not have. Write be_greater_than(x), be_greater_than_or_equal_to(x), be_less_than(x), be_less_than_or_equal_to(x), or be_between(minimum, maximum).")
end

struct BooleanMatcher
  wanted

  def matches?(actual)
    actual == wanted
  end

  def failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{inspect(wanted)}, got #{inspect(actual)}"
  end

  def negated_failure_message(actual)
    "expected anything but #{inspect(wanted)}"
  end
end

def be_true
  BooleanMatcher.new(wanted: true)
end

def be_false
  BooleanMatcher.new(wanted: false)
end

# The predicate matchers are one struct apiece, and they differ by exactly
# one method call. This is what rspec's `be_empty` / `be_nil` / `be_whatever`
# buys with `method_missing`, written out — the repetition is the honest cost
# of having no runtime dynamism, and the shape a compile-time macro (#14)
# would generate. Five of them is tolerable; forty would not be, which is the
# argument for the macro when a real file asks.
struct EmptyMatcher
  word

  def matches?(actual)
    actual.empty?
  end

  def failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{word}, got #{inspect(actual)}"
  end

  def negated_failure_message(actual)
    "expected not #{word}, got #{inspect(actual)}"
  end
end

def be_empty
  EmptyMatcher.new(word: "empty")
end

struct NilMatcher
  word

  def matches?(actual)
    actual.nil?
  end

  def failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{word}, got #{inspect(actual)}"
  end

  def negated_failure_message(actual)
    "expected not #{word}, got #{inspect(actual)}"
  end
end

def be_nil
  NilMatcher.new(word: "nil")
end

struct SomeMatcher
  word

  def matches?(actual)
    actual.some?
  end

  def failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{word}, got #{inspect(actual)}"
  end

  def negated_failure_message(actual)
    "expected not #{word}, got #{inspect(actual)}"
  end
end

def be_some
  SomeMatcher.new(word: "some")
end

# `be_nan` (#252): a NaN equals nothing, itself included, so `eq` can never
# check for one. The fifth predicate matcher.
struct NanMatcher
  word

  def matches?(actual)
    actual.nan?
  end

  def failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{word}, got #{inspect(actual)}"
  end

  def negated_failure_message(actual)
    "expected not #{word}, got #{inspect(actual)}"
  end
end

def be_nan
  NanMatcher.new(word: "NaN")
end

struct FailureMatcher
  word

  def matches?(actual)
    actual.failure?
  end

  def failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{word}, got #{inspect(actual)}"
  end

  def negated_failure_message(actual)
    "expected not #{word}, got #{inspect(actual)}"
  end
end

def be_failure
  FailureMatcher.new(word: "a failure")
end

# `include` is a keyword (ADR 0028 spends it on traits), so rspec's
# `expect(list).to include(3)` is unspellable and `contain` is the Portland
# word for it.
struct ContainMatcher
  wanted

  def matches?(actual)
    actual.include?(wanted)
  end

  def failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{inspect(actual)} to contain #{inspect(wanted)}"
  end

  def negated_failure_message(actual)
    "expected #{inspect(actual)} not to contain #{inspect(wanted)}"
  end
end

def contain(wanted)
  ContainMatcher.new(wanted: wanted)
end

struct KeyMatcher
  key

  def matches?(actual)
    actual.key?(key)
  end

  def failure_message(actual)
    "expected a key #{inspect(key)} in #{inspect(actual)}"
  end

  def negated_failure_message(actual)
    "expected no key #{inspect(key)} in #{inspect(actual)}"
  end
end

def have_key(wanted)
  KeyMatcher.new(key: wanted)
end

struct LengthMatcher
  expected

  def matches?(actual)
    actual.length == expected
  end

  def failure_message(actual)
    "expected length #{inspect(expected)}, got #{inspect(actual.length)}"
  end

  def negated_failure_message(actual)
    "expected any length but #{inspect(expected)}"
  end
end

def have_length(count)
  LengthMatcher.new(expected: count)
end
